<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paris</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Parisian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paris is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and has long been one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s principal political and cultural centres. It was the seat of government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> and the site of treaties such as the Treaty of Paris of 1763, which confirmed British colonial dominance. Its university was an early centre for the study of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>, and the last Brazilian emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a>, died in exile there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paris.jpg" alt="Paris" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yann Caradec from Paris, France · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Tour_Eiffel_vue_de_la_Tour_Saint-Jacques,_Paris_ao%C3%BBt_2014_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Levon V was given safe passage and went to Castile to seek John I's help, moved to France and died in exile in <b>Paris</b> in 1393; in 1396 his title passed to his cousin James I of Cyprus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…interest in seeing the monarchy outlive him and made no effort to defend it, died in exile in <b>Paris</b> in 1891 having presided over one of the most stable and constitutional governments of nineteenth-century Latin America — a ruler whose personal virtues outlasted his political will to govern.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…reunion of all the Frankish kingdoms under Charles the Fat (884–887) proved hollow: during the Viking siege of <b>Paris</b> (885–886) he bought the attackers off rather than fight, an act widely seen as cowardice, and he was deposed in November 887 and died in January 888, after which…</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>It sent James M. Mason to London and John Slidell to <b>Paris</b>, but neither won recognition or military aid.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…by Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk, Edvard Beneš and the Slovak astronomer and general Milan Rastislav Štefánik, whose Czechoslovak National Council in <b>Paris</b> won Allied recognition in 1918, helped by the fame of the Czechoslovak Legion, which had fought its way along the Trans-Siberian Railway during the Russian Civil War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The duchy was a largely rural land of grain fields, forests and vineyards, crossed by the Saône and by routes linking <b>Paris</b> and Champagne with Lyon, the Alpine passes and Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
